{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VknsZnSCWB2NSQvotqSixL","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd015-basename\n     2→title: \"cmd/basename: Strip Directory and Suffix from Filenames\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Shell scripts extract filename components from paths using basename. The macOS BSD\n     6→  basename differs from GNU basename in multi-argument support (-a flag) and NUL-delimited\n     7→  output (-z flag). The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n     8→  (gbasename, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations and edge\n     9→  cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Strip the directory component from one or more file paths, outputting only the final path component.\n    13→  - G2: Support optional suffix removal from the result.\n    14→  - G3: Support multi-argument mode (-a) for processing multiple paths in a single invocation.\n    15→  - G4: Support NUL-delimited output (-z) for safe handling of filenames containing newlines.\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Single-argument mode (default)\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: \"When invoked as 'basename NAME', must strip the longest prefix ending in '/' from NAME and print the result followed by a newline.\"\n    22→      - R1.2: \"When invoked as 'basename NAME SUFFIX', must strip the directory prefix and then remove SUFFIX from the end of the result if it matches. SUFFIX is a literal string match, not a pattern.\"\n    23→      - R1.3: Must strip trailing slashes from NAME before processing. Multiple consecutive trailing slashes are all removed.\n    24→      - R1.4: \"When NAME consists entirely of slashes, must output '/'.\"\n    25→      - R1.5: When NAME is an empty string, must output an empty line (newline only).\n    26→\n    27→  R2:\n    28→    title: Multi-argument mode (-a, --multiple)\n    29→    items:\n    30→      - R2.1: -a or --multiple must enable processing of multiple NAME arguments. Each NAME is processed independently and the result is printed on a separate line.\n    31→      - R2.2: -s SUFFIX or --suffix=SUFFIX must remove SUFFIX from each result and implies -a. When -s is given, the second positional argument is treated as a NAME, not a SUFFIX.\n    32→      - R2.3: In multi-argument mode without -s, no suffix removal is performed on any argument.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Output formatting and exit codes\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: Each result must be followed by a newline character, or by a NUL byte (0x00) if -z or --zero is given.\n    38→      - R3.2: Must exit 0 on success.\n    39→      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when called with an incorrect number of arguments in single-argument mode (zero arguments or more than two without -a).\n    40→      - R3.4: Must print an error message to stderr when argument validation fails.\n    41→\n    42→  R4:\n    43→    title: Differential testing\n    44→    items:\n    45→      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n    46→      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: simple path (dir/file), trailing slashes, root path (/), empty string, suffix removal, multi-argument mode (-a), suffix mode (-s), and NUL-delimited output (-z).\"\n    47→      - R4.3: Tests must verify error output and exit code for invalid argument counts.\n    48→\n    49→non_goals:\n    50→  - cmd/basename does not implement pattern-based suffix removal (glob or regex); suffix is a literal string match.\n    51→  - cmd/basename does not read from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n    52→  - cmd/basename does not handle encoding; paths are treated as byte sequences with '/' as the separator.\n    53→\n    54→acceptance_criteria:\n    55→  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/basename /usr/bin/sort produces 'sort', matching gbasename.\"\n    56→  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/basename include/stdio.h .h produces 'stdio', matching gbasename.\"\n    57→  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/basename -a /usr/bin/sort /usr/bin/cat produces 'sort' and 'cat' on separate lines, matching gbasename -a.\"\n    58→  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/basename -s .h include/stdio.h include/stdlib.h produces 'stdio' and 'stdlib' on separate lines.\"\n    59→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gbasename passes for simple paths, trailing slashes, root, empty string, suffix removal, -a, -s, and -z modes.\"\n    60→  - \"AC6: cmd/basename compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    61→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a9d5fb3-6be8-4467-833b-a377d2483dda","uuid":"6125219b-83d0-4009-a7e2-cefd9d5ad2b1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/333/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd015-basename.yaml","content":"id: prd015-basename\ntitle: \"cmd/basename: Strip Directory and Suffix from Filenames\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Shell scripts extract filename components from paths using basename. The macOS BSD\n  basename differs from GNU basename in multi-argument support (-a flag) and NUL-delimited\n  output (-z flag). The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n  (gbasename, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations and edge\n  cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Strip the directory component from one or more file paths, outputting only the final path component.\n  - G2: Support optional suffix removal from the result.\n  - G3: Support multi-argument mode (-a) for processing multiple paths in a single invocation.\n  - G4: Support NUL-delimited output (-z) for safe handling of filenames containing newlines.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Single-argument mode (default)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: \"When invoked as 'basename NAME', must strip the longest prefix ending in '/' from NAME and print the result followed by a newline.\"\n      - R1.2: \"When invoked as 'basename NAME SUFFIX', must strip the directory prefix and then remove SUFFIX from the end of the result if it matches. SUFFIX is a literal string match, not a pattern.\"\n      - R1.3: Must strip trailing slashes from NAME before processing. Multiple consecutive trailing slashes are all removed.\n      - R1.4: \"When NAME consists entirely of slashes, must output '/'.\"\n      - R1.5: When NAME is an empty string, must output an empty line (newline only).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Multi-argument mode (-a, --multiple)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -a or --multiple must enable processing of multiple NAME arguments. Each NAME is processed independently and the result is printed on a separate line.\n      - R2.2: -s SUFFIX or --suffix=SUFFIX must remove SUFFIX from each result and implies -a. When -s is given, the second positional argument is treated as a NAME, not a SUFFIX.\n      - R2.3: In multi-argument mode without -s, no suffix removal is performed on any argument.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Output formatting and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Each result must be followed by a newline character, or by a NUL byte (0x00) if -z or --zero is given.\n      - R3.2: Must exit 0 on success.\n      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when called with an incorrect number of arguments in single-argument mode (zero arguments or more than two without -a).\n      - R3.4: Must print an error message to stderr when argument validation fails.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: simple path (dir/file), trailing slashes, root path (/), empty string, suffix removal, multi-argument mode (-a), suffix mode (-s), and NUL-delimited output (-z).\"\n      - R4.3: Tests must verify error output and exit code for invalid argument counts.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/basename does not implement pattern-based suffix removal (glob or regex); suffix is a literal string match.\n  - cmd/basename does not read from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n  - cmd/basename does not handle encoding; paths are treated as byte sequences with '/' as the separator.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/basename /usr/bin/sort produces 'sort', matching gbasename.\"\n  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/basename include/stdio.h .h produces 'stdio', matching gbasename.\"\n  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/basename -a /usr/bin/sort /usr/bin/cat produces 'sort' and 'cat' on separate lines, matching gbasename -a.\"\n  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/basename -s .h include/stdio.h include/stdlib.h produces 'stdio' and 'stdlib' on separate lines.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gbasename passes for simple paths, trailing slashes, root, empty string, suffix removal, -a, -s, and -z modes.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/basename compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":61,"startLine":1,"totalLines":61}}}
