{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tJUj2JWR1KhhjPSjgi8Zh","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd016-dirname\n     2→title: \"cmd/dirname: Strip Last Component from File Paths\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Shell scripts extract directory components from paths using dirname. The macOS BSD\n     6→  dirname processes only a single argument, while GNU dirname accepts multiple arguments\n     7→  and supports NUL-delimited output. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n     8→  reference binary (gdirname, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all argument\n     9→  combinations and edge cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Strip the last component from one or more file paths, outputting the directory portion.\n    13→  - G2: Handle edge cases (root, trailing slashes, no directory component) per POSIX and GNU behavior.\n    14→  - G3: Support NUL-delimited output (-z) for safe handling of directory names containing newlines.\n    15→\n    16→requirements:\n    17→  R1:\n    18→    title: Core path stripping behavior\n    19→    items:\n    20→      - R1.1: Must strip trailing slashes from NAME, then remove the last component (everything after the final '/') and print the result.\n    21→      - R1.2: \"When NAME contains no '/' after trailing-slash removal, must output '.' (the current directory).\"\n    22→      - R1.3: \"When NAME is '/' or consists entirely of slashes, must output '/'.\"\n    23→      - R1.4: Must strip trailing slashes from the result. If the result after stripping would be empty, output the root slash instead.\n    24→      - R1.5: Must process multiple NAME arguments, printing one result per argument.\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Output formatting\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: Each result must be followed by a newline character, or by a NUL byte (0x00) if -z or --zero is given.\n    30→      - R2.2: Results must be printed in the order the arguments are given, one per line.\n    31→\n    32→  R3:\n    33→    title: Exit codes and error handling\n    34→    items:\n    35→      - R3.1: Must exit 0 on success.\n    36→      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when called with no arguments. Must print an error message to stderr.\n    37→      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    38→\n    39→  R4:\n    40→    title: Differential testing\n    41→    items:\n    42→      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n    43→      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: simple path (dir/file), nested path (a/b/c), trailing slashes, root path (/), relative path with no directory (file.txt -> '.'), dot path (.), double-dot path (..), multiple arguments, and NUL-delimited output (-z).\"\n    44→      - R4.3: Tests must verify error output and exit code when no arguments are given.\n    45→\n    46→non_goals:\n    47→  - cmd/dirname does not resolve symlinks or canonicalize paths; it operates on the string representation only.\n    48→  - cmd/dirname does not read from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n    49→  - cmd/dirname does not handle encoding; paths are treated as byte sequences with '/' as the separator.\n    50→\n    51→acceptance_criteria:\n    52→  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/dirname /usr/bin/sort produces '/usr/bin', matching gdirname.\"\n    53→  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/dirname stdio.h produces '.', matching gdirname.\"\n    54→  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/dirname /usr/bin/ produces '/usr', matching gdirname (trailing slash stripped).\"\n    55→  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/dirname / produces '/', matching gdirname.\"\n    56→  - \"AC5: go run ./cmd/dirname dir1/file dir2/file produces 'dir1' and 'dir2' on separate lines, matching gdirname.\"\n    57→  - \"AC6: Differential test against gdirname passes for simple paths, nested paths, trailing slashes, root, relative paths, dots, multiple arguments, and -z mode.\"\n    58→  - \"AC7: cmd/dirname compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    59→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a9d5fb3-6be8-4467-833b-a377d2483dda","uuid":"fa16a0f2-6124-4fab-adcf-dfadc0590306","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/333/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd016-dirname.yaml","content":"id: prd016-dirname\ntitle: \"cmd/dirname: Strip Last Component from File Paths\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Shell scripts extract directory components from paths using dirname. The macOS BSD\n  dirname processes only a single argument, while GNU dirname accepts multiple arguments\n  and supports NUL-delimited output. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gdirname, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all argument\n  combinations and edge cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Strip the last component from one or more file paths, outputting the directory portion.\n  - G2: Handle edge cases (root, trailing slashes, no directory component) per POSIX and GNU behavior.\n  - G3: Support NUL-delimited output (-z) for safe handling of directory names containing newlines.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core path stripping behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must strip trailing slashes from NAME, then remove the last component (everything after the final '/') and print the result.\n      - R1.2: \"When NAME contains no '/' after trailing-slash removal, must output '.' (the current directory).\"\n      - R1.3: \"When NAME is '/' or consists entirely of slashes, must output '/'.\"\n      - R1.4: Must strip trailing slashes from the result. If the result after stripping would be empty, output the root slash instead.\n      - R1.5: Must process multiple NAME arguments, printing one result per argument.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Output formatting\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Each result must be followed by a newline character, or by a NUL byte (0x00) if -z or --zero is given.\n      - R2.2: Results must be printed in the order the arguments are given, one per line.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Exit codes and error handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must exit 0 on success.\n      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when called with no arguments. Must print an error message to stderr.\n      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: simple path (dir/file), nested path (a/b/c), trailing slashes, root path (/), relative path with no directory (file.txt -> '.'), dot path (.), double-dot path (..), multiple arguments, and NUL-delimited output (-z).\"\n      - R4.3: Tests must verify error output and exit code when no arguments are given.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/dirname does not resolve symlinks or canonicalize paths; it operates on the string representation only.\n  - cmd/dirname does not read from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n  - cmd/dirname does not handle encoding; paths are treated as byte sequences with '/' as the separator.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/dirname /usr/bin/sort produces '/usr/bin', matching gdirname.\"\n  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/dirname stdio.h produces '.', matching gdirname.\"\n  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/dirname /usr/bin/ produces '/usr', matching gdirname (trailing slash stripped).\"\n  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/dirname / produces '/', matching gdirname.\"\n  - \"AC5: go run ./cmd/dirname dir1/file dir2/file produces 'dir1' and 'dir2' on separate lines, matching gdirname.\"\n  - \"AC6: Differential test against gdirname passes for simple paths, nested paths, trailing slashes, root, relative paths, dots, multiple arguments, and -z mode.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/dirname compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":59,"startLine":1,"totalLines":59}}}
